<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Welf</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as House of Guelph, Welf dynasty, Welfs, Guelphs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Welf, or Guelph, is a European dynasty of Swabian and Bavarian origin. Its members included the dukes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-proud">Henry the Proud</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, whose rivalry with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> gave the Guelph name to a party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and Emperor Otto IV. From Henry the Lion descended the dukes of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> and kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from 1714 to 1837.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-welf.jpg" alt="House of Welf" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kaiser Torikka+elements from the work of Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_Brunswick-L%C3%BCneburg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…a stem duchy of the Empire it passed between families: the Ottonian kings gave it to relatives, the <b>Welfs</b> held it from 1070 with interruptions, and in 1180 Frederick Barbarossa deposed Henry the Lion and granted the duchy to Otto of Wittelsbach, whose family ruled Bavaria until 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Lothair of Supplinburg then became duke and, in 1125, king; his heir was his son-in-law Henry the Proud of the <b>Welf</b> family.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Through Conrad's daughter Agnes it passed to the <b>Welf</b> Henry of Brunswick, and in 1214 Emperor Frederick II granted it to Louis I of Bavaria of the house of Wittelsbach, whose son Otto II married the Welf heiress.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By the thirteenth century the city was splitting into factions, the <b>Guelphs</b>, who looked to the papacy and the Angevins, and the Ghibellines, who supported the emperors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Dante belonged to the <b>Guelphs</b>, who in central Italy's struggle with the Ghibellines generally favoured the papacy over the Holy Roman Emperor.</blockquote><a href="/work/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The siege was a decisive clash in the rivalry between the Hohenstaufen and the <b>Welfs</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-21">21 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
